  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 PM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nSimilar to yesterday, temperatures across the UP are climbing into \nthe high 50s and low 60s this afternoon, as highs are projected to \nreach 15-20F above normal in the low and mid 60s. Southeasterly \nsurface winds, currently at 5-15 mph, are building, and GOES Day \nCloud imagery shows low-level clouds with southeasterly motion below \nhigh-level, glaciating clouds with northwesterly motion. RAP surface \nanalysis shows a 997mb Colorado low deepening to 996mb as it tracks \nfrom eastern NE toward western MN over the past 2 hours. WPC surface \nanalysis has the warm front of this low draped across southern \nOntario, well north of the UP and Lake Superior, and dewpoints in \nthe mid-50s are advecting into the CWA. Some low radar returns are \nworking their way across the UP from west to east, and MRMS \nestimates that less than 0.10\" inch has fallen across the western \nreaches of the UP.\n\nThe aforementioned Colorado low goes through several iterations of \nstretching and splitting over the next few days, sending waves of \nprecipitation in our direction as the low morphs and re-forms. \nTonight's rainfall is focused on the northern head of the low, which \nthen splits from its parent throughout Tuesday, resulting in a brief \nlull in rainfall Tuesday morning. The remnant (southern) low lingers \nover Wisconsin for all of Wednesday, consolidating and deepening as \nit does so; this places the UP within the warm sector and in a good \nspot to receive continued dousing during the second half of \nWednesday. Early Thursday, this second Colorado low gathers steam \nand moves northeast, leaving the UP in CAA with wrap-around \nmoisture, leading to (relatively) light lake effect showers in \nnortherly and northwesterly wind belts, primarily in the western UP.\n\nFor the 48-hour period from tonight through Wednesday night at 7pm \nEST, the NBM shows 70-95% chance of receiving greater than 1\" of \nrainfall across much of the UP, with the highest probabilities \nfocused across the southern tier and in the Keweenaw. For this same \ntime period for greater than 2\" of rain, chances decrease to 10-45%, \nwith the highest chances again across the south and the Keweenaw. \nPer the LREF, PWAT values at times over the next two days reach 100% \nof climatology at 1.6\", and are otherwise between 1.2\" and 1.5\". \nThis is unlikely to be even close to realized as surface \nprecipitation since the LREF shows only up to 100 J/kg of MUCAPE for \nsome times during this period. Thus, a rumble of thunder may be \nheard and rainfall amounts on the higher end may be locally reached. \nGiven the ongoing drought and long duration of this event, flooding \nand heavy precipitation rates are not a concern at this time. Given \na very moist atmosphere due to antecedent rain, patchy fog looks to \nbe widespread tonight over much of the CWA tonight and tomorrow \nmorning.\n\nAs the Colorado low departs northeast throughout Thursday and CAA \nsets in, temperatures begin to drop and winds shift northerly. \nLingering moisture will allow lake effect showers to form, though \ntotals will be much less than the preceding 48 hours, on the order \nof 0.1\" - 0.3\". The highest totals will be seen across the \nnorthernwestern quadrant of the UP (Gogebic, Ontonagon, Houghton, \nKeweenaw, Baraga, and northern Marquette Counties). As low \ntemperatures approach normal in the low 30s Friday morning, lake \neffect showers may turn to lake effect flurries in areas of higher \nterrain. At this time, snow is anticipated to be restricted mostly \nto the Michigamme Highlands, for a brief period prior to sunrise on \nFriday morning. Highs are expected to settle back down into the near-\nnormal range (40s) for Friday, with lows in the high 20s and low \n30s, prior to ridging and slightly warmer temperatures returning \nduring the weekend.",
